  "worked_solution": "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{4}}{(-7{x}^{4})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
